Class Loader
Class Loader dynamically loads Java classes into the JVM at runtime.

The JVM first loads the Employee.class file into memory using a Class Loader, and then creates the object.

Benefits:
	• Loads classes on demand (lazy loading)
	• Saves memory
	• Supports dynamic loading
	• Provides security through class loading hierarchy
	• Enables modular applications and application servers


Types of Class Loaders
Java provides three built-in class loaders.
1. Bootstrap Class Loader
Parent of all class loaders.
Loads core Java classes.


java.lang.String
java.lang.System
java.util.ArrayList


System.out.println(String.class.getClassLoader());
null

Why?
Because Bootstrap Loader is implemented in native code and represented as null.




2. Platform (Extension) Class Loader
Loads JDK platform libraries.


java.sql
java.xml
java.naming


3. Application Class Loader
Loads classes from application's classpath.

Employee.class
Student.class
OrderService.class



