Parent Delegation Model
This is one of the most important interview questions.

Step 1
Application Loader receives request.
Step 2
Delegates to parent.


App Loader
 ↓
Platform Loader
 ↓
Bootstrap Loader


Step 3
Bootstrap finds String class.
Step 4
Returns class.
Why?
Security.
Your application cannot replace:


java.lang.String
java.lang.System
java.lang.Object

With malicious version


The class loading hierarchy provides security through the Parent Delegation Model. Before loading a class, a class loader delegates the request to its parent. Since core Java classes are loaded by the Bootstrap Class Loader first, application code cannot override or replace trusted system classes like java.lang.String, thereby protecting the JVM from malicious class spoofing and ensuring class integrity.




Encapsulation is an OOP principle in which data and methods are wrapped into a single unit (class), and data is hidden using private access modifiers while providing controlled access through getters and setters.
 This improves security and maintainability. 

Benefits:
	• Data security
	• Controlled access to data
	• Better maintainability


This is encapsulation because the internal data is protected and can only be accessed through controlled methods.

Data Hiding is the practice of restricting direct access to an object's data (variables) and allowing access only through controlled methods.
It is achieved using access modifiers (private, protected, public, default).

Why Data Hiding?
	• Protects data from unauthorized access.
	• Prevents accidental modification of data.
	• Improves security and maintainability.
	• Supports encapsulation.

Data Hiding	Encapsulation
Restricts direct access to data.	Bundles data and methods into a single unit (class).
Achieved mainly using private access modifier.	Achieved using classes, private fields, and public methods.
Focuses on security.	Focuses on data security and abstraction.


Immutable 
An Immutable Class is a class whose objects cannot be modified after they are created.


Rules to Create an Immutable Class
	1. Declare the class as final so it cannot be extended.
	2. Make all fields private and final.
	3. Do not provide setter methods.
	4. Initialize fields through a constructor.
	5. Return copies of mutable objects instead of directly returning references.

String is immutable, and concat() creates a new String object containing the modified value.

An immutable object cannot be modified after creation.
Benefits:
	• Thread-safe
	• Secure
	• Easy caching
Why is String immutable?
	• Security
	• Thread safety
	• String pool optimization
	• Hashcode caching




