Idempotency means that performing the same operation multiple times produces the same result as performing it once.


idempotency works by associating each request with a unique Idempotency Key. When the server receives a request, it first checks whether that key has already been processed. If not, it executes the operation and stores the result against the key. If the same request is received again with the same key, the server returns the previously stored response instead of executing the operation again, thereby preventing duplicate processing.


Abstract Class

An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated (you cannot create its object directly). It is used to provide a common base class for other classes.
Key Points
	• Declared using the abstract keyword.
	• Can have abstract methods (methods without implementation).
	• Can have concrete methods (methods with implementation).
	• Can have constructors, variables, and static methods.
	• A subclass must implement all abstract methods, otherwise the subclass must also be declared abstract.


Abstract Method
An abstract method is a method that has only a declaration and no implementation. It must be implemented by the child class.

Key Points
	• Abstract methods are declared using the abstract keyword.
	• They do not have a method body.
	• They can only exist inside an abstract class (or interface).
	• Child classes must provide the implementation; otherwise, the child class must also be declared abstract.

An abstract method is a method without implementation that defines a contract for subclasses, forcing them to provide their own implementation.








