Garbage Collector (GC) in Java
Garbage Collector (GC) is a JVM mechanism that automatically frees memory by removing objects that are no longer reachable or used by the application.

Automatic memory management.
Removes unreachable objects.


Employee emp = new Employee();

Every object created would remain in memory forever unless manually deleted.
Java automatically identifies unused objects and reclaims their memory.


Garbage Collection is the JVM's automatic memory management process that identifies unreachable objects in the heap and reclaims their memory, helping prevent memory leaks and reducing the need for manual memory management. 


Minor GC
Occurs in Young Generation.
Fast.

Major GC
Cleans Old Generation.
Slower.

Full GC
Entire heap cleaned.
Most expensive.


Common Collectors
Serial GC
Single-threaded.

Parallel GC
Uses multiple threads

How do objects become eligible for GC?
	• Null reference
	• Reassigned reference
	• Out of scope
	• Anonymous objects

Can GC remove objects with references?
No.
Reachable objects are not collected.


Memory Management 

Memory Management in Java is the process by which the JVM allocates and deallocates memory during program execution. Java performs automatic memory management using the Garbage Collector (GC), which removes objects that are no longer reachable.
Java memory is mainly divided into:
	1. Stack Memory
		○ Stores local variables and method calls.
		○ Thread-specific.
		○ Automatically cleared when a method completes.
	2. Heap Memory
		○ Stores objects and instance variables.
		○ Shared among threads.
		○ Managed by the Garbage Collector.
	3. Metaspace (Method Area)
		○ Stores class metadata, static variables, and method information
		
Metaspace is the memory area in the JVM that stores class metadata such as:
	• Class definitions
	• Method metadata
	• Field metadata
	• Runtime constant pool
	• Static variables (associated with class metadata)
	• Annotations
Metaspace was introduced in Java 8 and replaced PermGen (Permanent Generation).

When Employee.class is loaded:
	• Object instances → go to Heap
	• Local variables → go to Stack
	• Class metadata of Employee → goes to Metaspace

Difference Between PermGen and Metaspace
PermGen	Metaspace
Used until Java 7	Introduced in Java 8
Part of JVM Heap	Uses Native Memory
Fixed size by default	Dynamically grows
OutOfMemoryError: PermGen space	OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace
Difficult to tune	Better memory management
Stores class metadata	Stores class metadata


Q: Where are objects stored?
Answer: Heap Memory.
Q: Where are local variables stored?
Answer: Stack Memory.
Q: What is stored in Metaspace?
Answer: Class metadata, method information, and static variables.
Q: Who manages heap memory?
Answer: JVM Garbage Collector.
Q: Difference between Stack and Heap?
Answer:
	• Stack stores local variables and method calls.
	• Heap stores objects.
	• Stack is automatically managed.
	• Heap is managed by the Garbage Collector.


Java Memory Model (JMM)
The Java Memory Model (JMM) defines how threads interact with memory and ensures visibility of shared variables.

JMM Concepts
Visibility
When Thread A updates a variable, Thread B should see the latest value.
Atomicity
Operation completes entirely or not at all.
Ordering
Instructions execute in expected order.

Java Memory Model defines the rules for visibility, atomicity, and ordering of shared data between threads. It ensures consistent behavior in concurrent applications using constructs like synchronized, volatile, and locks.


Volatile guarantees visibility of variable updates across threads and prevents instruction reordering, but it does not make compound operations atomic.

synchronized	ReentrantLock
Keyword	Class
Automatic lock release	Manual release
Simpler	More flexible
No timeout	Supports timeout
No fairness	Fair locking supported


